Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever a customer returns an item for a change of mind, or gives a blank or meaningless reason such as "because" (anything other than damaged), refund the amount actually paid for the returned items after the discount code, and never refund the shipping fee. Count days from delivery, not purchase. If the return is within 14 days of delivery (day 14 included), refund the full amount. If it is from day 15 to day 30, refund half. If it is after day 30, refuse the return and refund nothing of any kind, including store credit.
  2. Whenever a customer reports the goods as damaged within 90 days of delivery, refund the amount actually paid for the items after the discount code, plus the shipping cost. Whenever the damage is reported after 90 days, refund nothing. The change-of-mind timing rules do not apply to damaged goods.
  3. Whenever a refund is approved, return the money the way the customer paid, whatever the reason for the refund (including damaged goods). Card-paid orders are refunded to the card, and store-credit-paid orders are refunded as store credit.
  4. Whenever a refund amount has a fractional rupee part, round the refunded amount down to the nearest whole rupee (for example ₹749.80 is refunded as ₹749). This applies to every refund, whether paid by card or store credit, and for any reason.
  5. Whenever a refund is calculated, base it on the amount the customer paid, and never refund more than the customer paid.
  6. Whenever an order is marked final sale and its goods did not arrive damaged, refund nothing for the final-sale item. Other items in the order are refunded under the usual timing rules, with the amount worked out after the discount code. Shipping is not refunded unless the goods arrived damaged. If the final-sale goods arrived damaged, the damaged-goods rule applies. There is no rule for gift cards or gift card lines.
  7. Whenever an order is paid by card, award loyalty points once, when the order is placed. Award one point for every full ₹100 paid for the items after the discount code (rounding down), and shipping charges earn no points (for example, ₹2,350 of items plus ₹100 shipping earns 23 points). Whenever the order is final sale, award double points. Whenever the order is paid with store credit, award no points. Every customer earns at the same flat rate, with no club-membership, promotional or festival bonus.
  8. Whenever an order is cancelled or refunded, do not change the loyalty points already earned. There is no rule that takes points back (Ravi keeps his 30 points).
  9. Whenever today's revenue is calculated, add up the total of every card-paid order only, where an order's total is its items after the discount code plus shipping. Store-credit orders contribute nothing to revenue. The average is revenue divided by the number of card-paid orders, rounded down, or 0 if there are no card orders. The orders count includes every order in the list, store-credit ones too. There is no rule for cancelled, refunded or unpaid orders.
  10. If a card-paid order's items come to ₹500 or more in total but one of the items is priced under ₹500, does the order earn loyalty points? -> Neither. The rule is on the order's total: if the items come to ₹500 or more after the discount code, the order earns points, and the price of each single item doesn't matter. So A, with one detail: store-credit orders earn none, and final-sale orders earn double.
